Windows Server 2016
Data Center Edition.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.934
So we launched five days after
they [INAUDIBLE] the bits.

00:11:38.934 --> 00:11:40.100
We're very happy to do that.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.080
We've been in very
close partnership.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:44.330
So we have the latest and
greatest Windows Server

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.560
versions on Google Cloud.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.860
MARK MANDEL: Since we're
talking about Windows versions,

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.480
I know we also
support SQL Server.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.200
What versions of SQL
Server do we support?

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.158
AMRUTA GULANIKAR: Yes,
that's a great question.

00:11:56.158 --> 00:11:58.412
So we support the
different editions

00:11:58.412 --> 00:11:59.370
and different versions.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.760
So we definitely have
support for standard web

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.020
and express all in GA.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.930
We are actively working on
getting SQL Server Enterprise

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:07.430
out.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:09.347
It's already in alpha.

00:12:09.347 --> 00:12:11.180
We're testing it with
a couple of customers.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:14.459
And we have a wide
range of versions

00:12:14.459 --> 00:12:17.000
along with the additions I just
mentioned, so we have support

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.330
from 2012, 2014, and the
latest and greatest, 2016.

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.080
We also support SQL Server
2016 on Windows Server

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.160
2016 as of last week.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:29.000
So we are pretty excited to
have a really broad range

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.370
of editions and
versions that people

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.782
can choose to spin up on us.

00:12:33.782 --> 00:12:35.090
FRANCESC COMPOY: Cool.

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:41.030
So then if I'm a Windows
developer with a C#

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:45.890
application, I can deploy a
new Windows instance and deploy

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:49.820
my code into that one,
and start running it.

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:51.590
Can I run it on Linux, too?

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:54.470
Could you explain what is the
difference between running a C#

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.330
application on Linux or Windows?

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:59.030
I know that C# now
runs on Linux, too,

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:00.840
because it's open-source.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:04.212
CHRIS SELLS: Yeah, so we're
focused a lot on this,

00:13:04.212 --> 00:13:05.270
on the tooling side.

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:10.670
Of course we want to make sure
that people coming to Google

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:12.410
have a great experience
on our machines,

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.060
and we work hard
to make sure we've

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:18.560
got wonderful distributions
that run great on our platform

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.980
for both Linux and
Windows Server.

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:26.780
And our tutorials know that 99%
of the world out there today

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.720
is using ASP.NET, they're
targeting Windows,

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:32.240
and so it's ASP.NET 4.x.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:35.090
And of course, we built all
our tools and our tutorials

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:36.080
all around that, right?

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:40.100
Making sure that you can deploy
to Windows Server instances

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:43.040
running ASP.NET and
IAS the way you expect.

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:48.740
That said, we're also working
really hard on supporting .NET

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.950
Core, which is the
cross-platform version of .NET.

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:55.550
It's kind of the follow-on
to the .NET that is

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:58.130
Windows-specific that
Microsoft has released

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:02.660
into the open-source and that
we've been working to support

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:04.660
on our cloud.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.770
And in fact, if you
go to the GCP blog,

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:09.710
there have been some recent
articles about how to take

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:15.590
your .NET Core code and make it
work on App Engine Flex on GCP

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:20.060
and on Container Engine
on GCP using Kubernetes.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:24.560
And so the way that works is
you take your .NET Core code

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:28.980
and you package it up with a
base Docker image that supports

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.860
the .NET Core runtime.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:33.020
And then you can bundle
all that together

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:35.450
and you can deploy it to App
Engine Flex or Kubernetes

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:39.430
running on Container Engine.

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:40.580
So you can do that today.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.110
There's nothing stopping you.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:45.260
The set of libraries
that we support,

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:48.870
we're working hard to make
sure that you can do all that.

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:53.150
All the code you want to write
against our libraries works

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.760
in the Windows version
of .NET, of course,

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:59.750
and that's 200 plus APIs
across all of Google.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:01.645
This is not just BigQuery
and Cloud Storage,

00:15:01.645 --> 00:15:03.020
although, of
course, we do those,

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.430
too, all the cloud stuff.

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:08.090
But it's also locations
and maps and, you know,

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:11.090
G-Drive and Docs and
all the wonderful APIs

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:13.610
that people use across Google.

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:19.130
We have support for that
100% inside of Windows.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:23.420
And that same set of APIs, we
have about 99% coverage right

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:24.770
now for .NET Core.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:26.810
It's not quite 100%.

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:32.180
We're busy updating our
GRPC implementation, which

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:33.920
is based around HTTP2.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:36.710
It's binary, it's
streaming, it's faster.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:41.960
And the libraries that require
that protocol don't yet work

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.210
for .NET Core, but
we're so close.

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:45.940
We're so close to
making that work.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.760
So I expect very soon--
hopefully by the end

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:54.590
of the year, certainly by Q1--
that 100% of all of our APIs

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:55.920
will work in .NET Core, too.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:59.810
Which means that you can,
in Visual Studio or VS Code

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:04.430
on Linux, Mac, or Windows, write
your .NET Core application,

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:09.170
you can use Nougat to bring in
the Google .NET Core libraries,

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:13.520
write your code and deploy it
to App Engine Flexible Edition

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.130
or Container Engine
running Kubernetes,

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:19.102
and it'll just work.

00:16:19.102 --> 00:16:20.435
MARK MANDEL: That's pretty cool.

00:16:20.435 --> 00:16:23.480
All right, so I'm going to ask
a question that I know-- well,

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:26.570
so I do some game
development stuff.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.899
Any idea if it'll work
in Unity, the C# stuff?

00:16:29.899 --> 00:16:31.190
CHRIS SELLS: Ah, good question.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:34.790
Unity is an open question
for us because, of course,

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.110
we want to support Unity.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:42.445
Unity has a fabulous library
environment for doing

00:16:42.445 --> 00:16:45.570
.NET-based development.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.360
So we want to make
sure we support it.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:52.520
Unfortunately, right
now, Unity is .NET 2.x.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:57.710
And they're moving to
.NET 4.x with Mono.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:01.430
So as soon as they've moved
there and we have a reasonable

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:05.060
platform, because I admit, we
don't support .NET all the way

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:05.952
back to 2.0.

00:17:05.952 --> 00:17:07.535
MARK MANDEL: Not
many people do, which

00:17:07.535 --> 00:17:09.200
is why I asked the question.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.319
CHRIS SELLS: But when Unity
comes along with a more modern

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:15.829
version of .NET, then we're
going to see what we can do

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:18.710
about supporting it because
we want Unity games to be able

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:21.770
to use the Google Cloud
Platform, as well.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.339
MARK MANDEL: That
makes me happy.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:25.790
FRANCESC COMPOY: Cool,
so if I'm not mistaken,

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.460
everything that we've been
talking about for containers

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:34.050
was on Linux containers.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:35.430
What about Windows containers?

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:39.580
Because I've read
somewhere that they exist.

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:40.860
AMRUTA GULANIKAR: Definitely.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.850
Windows containers is
definitely interesting,

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.160
and we are keeping
a close eye on it.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:48.050
The step 1 in actually
supporting Windows Containers

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:51.370
is to support Windows Server
2016, which we have done.

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:56.060
We're also working very actively
on supporting Windows Nano

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:57.690
images.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:02.510
So this would help
accelerate people actually

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:05.690
being able to deploy these
container images on a smaller

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:07.550
footprint of Windows,
which is Windows Nano

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:12.890
2016, the preferred
OS for data centers,

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:15.740
much faster, smaller
footprint, so on and so forth.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:18.950
So we're actively building
the support in the platform

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:21.530
first, so we're definitely
working on that.

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:24.290
In parallel, we're deeply
engaged with the community

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:28.070
on, sort of, building support
for Windows containers

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:29.510
in Kubernetes.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:34.310
So you will probably see some
interesting things come out

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:35.610
there pretty soon.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:38.090
And we're sort of
waiting and watching

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:41.460
and kind of looking at the
customer adoption for Windows

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:41.980
containers.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.104
There are pretty new and
shiny, and a lot of people

00:18:44.104 --> 00:18:47.090
are excited about it, so we're
keeping a close pulse on that.

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:50.140
And we definitely want
to take that feedback

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:52.660
as we kind of think
about building support

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:55.255
in GKE for that.

00:18:55.255 --> 00:18:58.580
But I think, to be very
honest, we are kind of

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:01.480
wanting to wait and see how
people start using our Windows

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:05.625
2016 images, Nano
images, and then

00:19:05.625 --> 00:19:08.000
the excitement in the Kubernetes
community around Windows

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.310
containers are definitely
going to be things that help

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:14.570
us decide what to do next here.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:16.070
FRANCESC COMPOY:
So for people that

00:19:16.070 --> 00:19:18.320
don't know that much
about Windows containers,

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.660
other than they
exist-- like me--

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:21.860
what is the main difference?

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:23.960
Is there a
technological difference

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:28.050
on the type of container it
is, or is it just a base image,

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:29.930
which is completely different?

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:32.960
CHRIS SELLS: So here's how
to think about it, kind of,

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:33.800
in the big picture.

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:36.830
If you think about the
idea of Linux containers

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:42.640
as being kind of a
lighter-weight containment

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:44.810
protocol for your
applications, where

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:48.800
you can pack multiple containers
on top of a single VM that

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:52.190
have access to shared resources
like the operating system

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:55.640
image and the hard drive,
but these are still

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:58.600
environments that are
isolated from each other,

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:00.350
the idea, of course,
is that you can

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.120
stack multiple of
these containers

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:04.880
into a single virtual
machine and get the most

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:08.750
out of the resources
of that machine

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:14.180
without having to open
up the hole of having

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:16.520
these things break
their boundaries

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.960
and talk to each other or try
to crack or hack each other.

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:23.300
If you think about that,
well, Linux containers

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:26.620
are just containers that
have a shared Linux base,

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:29.480
and your code runs assuming
Linux is under there.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:33.030
And Windows containers is just
your Windows code in a Windows

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:36.710
container that has a shared
access to the set of resources

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:37.880
inside of Windows.

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:40.370
So while conceptually
they are the same,

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:44.720
of course your code has to
run on Linux or Windows,

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:48.090
and you have to think about
it differently and write

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:49.340
your code differently.

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:52.320
But the nice thing is that
Microsoft has worked hard

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:56.040
with Docker to make sure that
the same set of tools that you

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.770
use for managing
Linux containers

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:01.700
can also be used to
manage Windows containers.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:04.611
Which means that from
a tooling point of view

00:21:04.611 --> 00:21:06.110
and an orchestration
point of view--

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:08.580
for example, that's what
Kubernetes is all about,

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:10.640
right, orchestrating
containers--

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.160
you can think of about
them in the same way,

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:14.174
which is really nice.

00:21:14.174 --> 00:21:15.090
FRANCESC COMPOY: Nice.

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:15.585
Thank you.

00:21:15.585 --> 00:21:15.970
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:16.900
I think that's great.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:20.400
All right, so I think it's
time-- this is the Google Cloud

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:23.607
Platform Podcast where
we ask the hard questions

00:21:23.607 --> 00:21:25.940
and we talk a little bit about
the elephant in the room.

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:28.260
More often than not,
when people think

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:33.570
of running Windows in the cloud,
they think of Microsoft Azure.

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:35.520
Why should people
come to us instead?

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:37.910
Like, is there, like,
philosophical differences

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:39.579
or other differences
that they should

00:21:39.579 --> 00:21:41.870
be aware of when making a
decision on where they should

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:44.390
be running in the cloud?

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:48.410
CHRIS SELLS: Well, certainly
from the Windows world

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:53.140
and from the Microsoft
world even more,

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:57.810
both Amruta and I have some
kind of understanding of the way

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:00.300
that the Windows community
thinks about these things

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:03.030
and the, kind of,
open-source community

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.090
thinks about these things.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:07.830
And for a long time these
were different communities.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:10.590
The Windows people did it
the way Microsoft said,

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:15.510
and there was enterprises
that, kind of, were

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.900
encouraged to do
things a certain way

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:20.310
with certain set of tools.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.910
And here, over in
the Google world,

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:24.860
we're much more
open about things.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:29.060
We are heavily involved with
open-source and open standards.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:32.120
And so certainly
on Azure, you're

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:34.160
encouraged to do
things very much

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:38.520
the same old Microsoft way that
you've done them before, which

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:41.240
has its pluses and minuses.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:44.690
Here in the Google world,
we're much more open.

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:48.630
We're much less about
you come to our platform

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:49.740
and then you're locked in.

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:51.480
You can't go anywhere else.

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.061
For example, we were just
talking about containers,

00:22:54.061 --> 00:22:54.560
right?

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:57.350
The idea of a container is
you can run that container

00:22:57.350 --> 00:22:58.910
where you want it, right?

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:00.330
Where it runs best.

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:03.380
And because we've built
our Container Engine, which

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:09.780
is our orchestration platform
for containers on our platform,

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:13.740
based on open standards--
Docker containers,

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:16.700
Kubernetes-- we
very much encourage

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:19.050
you to bring your
container-based workloads

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:20.540
to our platform.

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:24.430
But if you don't like it here,
you can take them other places.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:27.150
Lots of people run Kubernetes
on other cloud providers,

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:30.170
lots of people run
Kubernetes on-premise,

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:33.110
and, in fact, lots of people
run Kubernetes between the cloud

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:35.660
and on-premise depending
on the resources they need

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:37.770
and the access they
need for their software,

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:40.130
and you can more easily
go back and forth.

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:46.370
And so at Google, instead of
to lock you into how you do

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:48.860
things, we try to
make sure that we're

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:54.590
the best place to run those open
things so that we try to earn

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:56.180
your business every day.

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:57.990
So that's one way of saying it.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:59.970
Another way of
saying it is we think

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:04.050
there's a bunch of stuff on our
platform that is just better.

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:08.100
We have more choices,
we have more options

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:12.540
to make it easier to spend less
money to run your stuff more

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:13.370
efficiently.

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:16.740
Of course we're the world
leader in big data and machine

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:21.300
learning, and of course you
get Google's amazing networking

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:23.580
and infrastructure when
you come to our platform.

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:30.200
So I think that the Google Cloud
Platform is a fabulous choice

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:32.955
whether you're a Linux developer
or a Windows developer.

00:24:32.955 --> 00:24:34.830
AMRUTA GULANIKAR: Yeah,
that's exactly right.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.430
And just to kind of chime
in, I couldn't agree more

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:39.620
with Chris over there.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:41.700
