00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:44.450
MARK MANDEL: So in our notes I
have a thing about PowerShell

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:46.251
and open-source.

00:25:46.251 --> 00:25:48.000
And I'm happy to admit
that I know nothing

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.130
about either of them.

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:51.380
So it sounds like there's
something interesting there.

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:53.730
Chris, do you want to
tell us what's going on?

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:55.813
CHRIS SELLS: Well, of
course you know a great deal

00:25:55.813 --> 00:25:58.380
about open-source, but I think
what you mean is PowerShell

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:00.270
plus open-source together.

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:01.710
I mean, let me back right up.

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:06.210
So the idea of PowerShell
is that it's the shell

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:10.080
that Windows developers and
IT administrators specifically

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:14.670
use because it is essentially
a really great way

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:18.890
to interactively understand
and manage various resources.

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:22.020
So for example, if I've
got a cloud provider that

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.060
provides a set of
commandlets, or if I've

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:29.370
got a piece of software
that has an administration

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:34.680
interface, by having
a set of commandlets,

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:39.030
I can, with a very easy set
of commands that I already

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:42.860
probably know, a set of standard
verbs across everything,

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:45.121
I can say I want to
be able to create

00:26:45.121 --> 00:26:46.620
an instance of this
resource, I want

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:49.370
to be the list, the set
of resources of this type,

00:26:49.370 --> 00:26:52.650
I want to be able to
remove them or update them.

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:55.440
You know, the standard
kind of CRUD style

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:57.240
is baked right
into the verbs that

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.090
are part of PowerShell,
which is great

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:02.630
because it makes it super
easy to use and learn

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:03.990
new sets of commandlets.

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:06.300
And now there are
tens of thousands

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:08.430
of these commandlets for
various pieces of software

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:10.971
in the world in the
Windows community.

00:27:10.971 --> 00:27:12.720
The other thing that
makes it really great

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:15.600
is as you interact
with these resources,

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:18.420
you get lists of
resources or lists

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:21.630
of what look like normal
lines like he would

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:25.950
in Bash or your
standard Unix shell,

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:27.930
but that's only
how it's rendered.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:30.240
What you're actually getting
back is sets of objects,

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.180
and you can pipe these objects
from one command to another.

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:35.280
And PowerShell is
really a language

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:36.660
for manipulating objects.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:40.160
What it means is that
unlike in the Unix world,

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:43.080
you don't have to do
regular expression matching

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:45.860
to be able to pull out various
bits and pieces of the data

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:51.060
as it goes by, you can just
look at object properties

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.550
and you can call object
methods in a way that

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:56.360
is very easy for
developers to understand.

00:27:56.360 --> 00:27:59.220
And IT administrators
have really

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:01.200
fallen in love with this.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:04.860
And so when I say that
Google Cloud Platform has

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:09.000
an initial set of commandlets
for a number of our most

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.160
popular resources, everyone
that does PowerShell and wants

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:16.100
to use it with
GCP is immediately

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:18.720
interested and excited
and understands

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:20.910
the benefits and power of that.

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:22.200
So that's the base.

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:25.110
Now, what does it mean when
we talk about PowerShell

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:25.810
and open-source?

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:28.830
Well, Microsoft has
been busy pushing

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:30.600
a lot of their tools
and technologies

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:32.340
into the open-source world.

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:38.710
C# is open-source, MSBuild,
Visual Studio Code.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:41.840
.NET itself has been
made open-source.

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:44.040
And one of the things
that they've recently

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:47.529
made open-source is PowerShell.

00:28:47.529 --> 00:28:49.320
And not only have they
made it open-source,

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:50.778
but they've made
it cross-platform.

00:28:50.778 --> 00:28:53.880
So you can, in fact, take
PowerShell, that same kind

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:56.790
of shell experience
with well-known verbs

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:59.700
in the piping of objects
from one command to another,

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:03.030
and you can run that in a
Linux world or on your Mac.

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:07.320
And so what that means, then,
is for all the commandments

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.240
to come along, the
commandlet providers

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:13.110
have to also support
versions of their commandlets

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:14.460
that are cross-platform.

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:18.140
And so when Microsoft made
this initial announcement

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:20.130
for an open-source,
cross-platform version

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:23.870
of PowerShell, they
reached out to us,

00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:26.820
and we've been
partnering with them.

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:28.920
And we're one of a
very small handful

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:31.590
of companies that are actually
on the leading edge here.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:34.590
We had advanced access
to these technologies,

00:29:34.590 --> 00:29:36.930
and we're well down
the path of making

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:43.680
our same set of commandlets
available to PowerShell

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:46.560
on Windows to making
them available for Linux

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:47.764
and the Mac, as well.

00:29:47.764 --> 00:29:48.680
FRANCESC COMPOY: Nice.

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.970
So it sounds like
we're in a world where

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:54.800
you can use PowerShell on
Linux and Bash on Windows.

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:56.360
CHRIS SELLS: Yeah.

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:57.554
Exactly right.

00:29:57.554 --> 00:29:59.220
FRANCESC COMPOY: That
is pretty amazing.

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:00.720
CHRIS SELLS: In
fact, if you wanted,

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:02.670
you could run Bash on
Windows and then use

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:05.730
