is executing, and also the
values of the variables

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:59.530
defined, which means that
you can basically have, like,

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:02.430
a snapshot of the whole
status of your server

00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:03.820
at the moment you care about.

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:04.450
MARK MANDEL: So it
should really be

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:06.460
called the Stackdriver,
like, Snapshotter.

00:35:06.460 --> 00:35:07.668
FRANCESC COMPOY: Snapshotter.

00:35:07.668 --> 00:35:10.250
That sounds actually
really cool.

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:14.530
Yeah, so the cool thing about
it is that it used to be beta,

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:15.720
it is now GA.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.650
So give it a try.

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:21.445
I know that I've used it in Go,
not for App Engine because Go

00:35:21.445 --> 00:35:23.870
on App Engine still doesn't
work, but we're working on it,

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:27.588
but it does work for Go
on Kubernetes-- well,

00:35:27.588 --> 00:35:29.480
on GKE and on GCE.

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:31.210
So if you have some
application there,

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:32.520
it actually works really well.

00:35:32.520 --> 00:35:34.270
MARK MANDEL: And if
you're a Java, Python,

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:37.570
or Node aficionado, it
works across Google App

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:40.330
Engine as well as Compute Engine
and Container Engine, as well.

00:35:40.330 --> 00:35:41.830
FRANCESC COMPOY:
So yeah, definitely

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:43.160
worth checking it out.

00:35:43.160 --> 00:35:45.040
Next time you have a
bug and you don't really

00:35:45.040 --> 00:35:50.260
